#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Proposing a change with a LIP"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "[LIP-1]{1} describes the process in full. In short:"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Describe the problem in a discussion first."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Copy the [template]{1} to `docs/lips/lip-draft-<short-name>.md`, fill in every section, and open a change. The editors assign a number."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Move it to Review when the text is complete. After that it goes to a 14-day Last Call, and then the Data Lead and the Technical Lead decide."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "If it's accepted, we record it as a [decision record]{1} and release it in a catalogue version."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Every LIP has a privacy and safeguarding section. Most of the data this blueprint governs is about children, so tell us what your change does to it."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Setting up"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Everything runs in the Nix dev shell, and every task is a `just` recipe. That way your tools are the same as everyone else's."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "If you use [direnv]{1}, run `direnv allow` once and it will enter the shell for you."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Making the change"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "**Edit the source, not the output.** Clause text, levels, verification and examples live in [`blueprint/catalogue.yaml`]{1}, and standards live in [`blueprint/standards.yaml`]{2}. `just render` writes everything between `BEGIN GENERATED` and `END GENERATED` markers, so edit the source and then run it."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "**Let the tools format.** The formatting and lint rules live in the configuration files at the root. Run `just fmt` rather than fixing the layout by hand. If you think a rule is wrong, change it in its config file, in a change of its own."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "**Use the domain language.** Each bounded context keeps its terms in its `CONTEXT.md`, and the [context map]{1} lists them all. Use the canonical term, and never a word listed under *Avoid*. If you settle a new term, add it to its context's `CONTEXT.md` in the same change."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "**Write clauses that can be checked.** A clause uses MUST, SHOULD or MAY in the sense of [BCP 14]{1}. It says what is required, not which product provides it, and its `verify` says how a test or a reviewer can check it. If the clause is abstract, add an `example`."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "**Cite standards where they're published.** Link to the publisher's own page for the edition you rely on, and add it to the standards register. Don't cite someone else's summary of it."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "**Write English that translates well.** Use short sentences and plain words, and don't put text in images. If you edit a page that has translations, run `just l10n-update` in the same change. The [translating]{1} guide explains why."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "**Name roles, not people.** Write \"the Data Lead\" or \"the steward of the learners domain\", never a person's name. Don't use real learners, staff or institutions in examples either."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Committing"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "The repository uses [jj]{1}, colocated with Git, but plain Git works too. Keep each change small and about one thing."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Commit messages follow the Angular convention, `type(scope): subject`. Write the subject in lowercase, in the imperative, in under 72 characters, and use the body to say *why* you made the change. The types are feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, build, ci, chore and revert. If your change breaks existing bindings, put `BREAKING CHANGE:` in the footer."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Review"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "A reviewer checks these things, in this order:"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Does `just check` pass?"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Did the change take the right path from the table above?"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Are the clauses testable and product-neutral, and do they use the domain language?"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Were generated sections regenerated rather than edited by hand?"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Does the change say what it does to learners' and staff members' data, and is that acceptable?"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Reviews follow Google's [engineering practices]{1}. In particular, a reviewer approves a change when it makes the blueprint better, even if it isn't perfect."
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "Reporting a security or safeguarding problem"
msgstr ""

#: CONTRIBUTING.md
msgid "If a problem could expose people's data, please don't open a public issue about it. Use the code host's private vulnerability reporting for this repository, or contact the maintainers privately."
msgstr ""

